  "result": "**Root cause:** `parseAmount` in `src/money.js` only matched plain digits. Prices with thousands separators, such as `\"2,450.50\"`, `\"1,299.00\"` and `\"1,005.99\"`, didn't match the regex. The match came back `null`, and `m[1]` threw `TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading '1')`, which surfaced as the 500. Every 500 I checked in the log (the first nine, plus the one at line 1608) had a comma-formatted price in its checkout payload on the preceding line. That's why the errors looked intermittent: only orders containing a price of 1,000 or more were affected.\n\n**Fix (`src/money.js`):**\n- The parser now accepts properly grouped thousands separators.\n- Anything that still isn't a valid amount throws `Invalid amount: <input>`, as the function's comment already said it should. A bad input now gives a clear error instead of a `TypeError`.\n\n**Tests (`test/money.test.js`):**\n- Comma-formatted amounts parse correctly.\n- Invalid amounts, including a badly grouped `\"1,23.00\"`, throw the clear error.\n- Checkout totals work with a comma-formatted price.\n\n`npm test` passes: 7 of 7. I didn't run the server or replay requests against it.\n\n**Not fixed:** an invalid price still throws, so `POST /checkout` would still return a 500 for it unless `src/server.js` turns that error into a 4xx. I didn't read `src/server.js`.",
